Equilibrium, state variables and local description (@th-state: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer, #source "../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1", #source "../sources/Advanced-Thermodynamics-MIT-2-43.lingua#r_5GXQQ35FEFJM34SWA12X8QHGKC", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "../../../inquiry/metrology/applications/Temperature-and-thermal-response.lingua#r_480AQX951V9NFA6AB86KJA1QZD") { r_7ZE016Z66H53QB69W5F3HVEN5X
**Read:** [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1), systems, equilibrium and phase chapters; [Advanced Thermodynamics — MIT 2.43, Spring 2024](../sources/Advanced-Thermodynamics-MIT-2-43.lingua#r_5GXQQ35FEFJM34SWA12X8QHGKC), state, equilibrium and nonequilibrium foundations.

An equilibrium description needs sufficient independent variables and the material/composition. For a simple single-phase compressible substance, two independent intensive properties often determine the state; at saturation, temperature and pressure are related and do not determine the liquid/vapour fraction. Count independent information before solving.

Equilibrium does not mean every property must be spatially uniform under all constraints. Gravity, interfaces and imposed fields can produce equilibrium variations. A steady flowing system can maintain unchanging profiles while generating entropy. A metastable phase may persist even when another state has a lower relevant potential.

Local equilibrium assigns a state relation to small regions within a nonuniform system. It is an approximation requiring appropriate length/time-scale separation, not a consequence of putting many cells in a mesh. Explain which internal variables, relaxation times or carriers must be added when that description fails.

